# Postgres container configuration — NOT API configuration.
#
# Copy to $APP_DIR/db/.env on the server, beside docker-compose.prod.yml. Docker
# Compose reads a file called `.env` from the compose file's own directory
# automatically, which is why `docker compose up -d` then needs no inline vars
# and — more importantly — why a later `docker compose up -d` or a host reboot
# cannot come back up with different credentials than the volume was created with.
#
# These three never go into ENV_API_BASE64 and are never seen by GitHub Actions.
# The API reaches Postgres through DATABASE_URL alone; it has no idea how the
# container was configured. You keep the two sides in sync by hand — each value
# below appears in exactly one place in the URL:
#
#   POSTGRES_USER  ─────────────┐
#   POSTGRES_PASSWORD  ─────────┼────┐
#   POSTGRES_DB  ───────────────┼────┼──────────────────┐
#                               v    v                  v
#   DATABASE_URL="postgresql://crossword:<password>@127.0.0.1:5007/crossword?schema=public"
#                                                                      ^^^^^^
#   (goes in server/.env.<env>.local)                    the DATABASE, not the user
#
# With the defaults below that is, literally:
#
#   DATABASE_URL="postgresql://crossword:<your-hex-password>@127.0.0.1:5007/crossword?schema=public"
#
# `crossword` twice is correct — the role and the database happen to share a name.
# `?schema=public` is a different thing again: the schema INSIDE that database.
# Prisma defaults to `public`; leave it alone.
#
# 127.0.0.1, never `localhost` — it can resolve to ::1, where nothing listens.
# 5007 is the HOST port this container publishes; inside it Postgres is still on
# 5432, and the target server already runs something else on 5432.
#
# Dev publishes 5007 too, so a dev and a production DATABASE_URL differ only by
# host and password. `127.0.0.1` vs `localhost` is the tell.
#
# ⚠ POSTGRES_PASSWORD is only read on FIRST start, when the data directory is
# initialised. Changing it later does nothing — the volume keeps the original.
# To rotate, change it inside Postgres instead:
#   docker exec -it crossword-postgres-prod psql -U crossword -c "ALTER USER crossword WITH PASSWORD 'new';"
# then update DATABASE_URL and redeploy.

POSTGRES_USER=crossword
# Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
# Hex on purpose — no characters that need percent-encoding inside DATABASE_URL.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
POSTGRES_DB=crossword
